explanation:
A **class** is a template for creating objects that bundle data with the code that works on it. `new ClassName(args)` creates an instance and runs the special `constructor` method to initialise it.

```js
class Stack {
  #items = [];                 // private field: only code in the class body can touch it
  push(value) { this.#items.push(value); return this; }
  pop() { return this.#items.pop(); }
  get size() { return this.#items.length; }
}
const s = new Stack().push(1).push(2);
console.log(s.pop(), s.size); // 2 1
```

Fields whose names start with `#` are **private**: it is an error to reference them from outside the class, so callers cannot depend on internals. Declare them up front in the class body.

`class Child extends Parent` creates a subclass. If the subclass has its own constructor, it must call `super(...)` before using `this`; `super.method()` calls the parent's version of a method. Unlike function declarations, classes are not hoisted — define a class before you use it.

steps:
Run it:
```js
import assert from "node:assert/strict";
class Stack {
  #items = [];
  push(value) { this.#items.push(value); return this; }
  pop() { return this.#items.pop(); }
  get size() { return this.#items.length; }
}
class BoundedStack extends Stack {
  constructor(capacity) {
    super();
    this.capacity = capacity;
  }
  push(value) {
    if (this.size >= this.capacity) throw new RangeError("stack is full");
    return super.push(value);
  }
}
const plates = new BoundedStack(2);
plates.push("a").push("b");
assert.throws(() => plates.push("c"), RangeError);
assert.equal(plates.pop(), "b");
assert.equal(plates.size, 1);
assert.equal(plates.items, undefined);   // #items is not a public property
console.log("size", plates.size);
```
